  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A million dollars is on offer to read five symbols. January 2025. Tamil Nadu's chief minister put up the prize.",
        "visual_query": "indus valley harappa excavated ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "excavated ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The Indus script was written between roughly 2800 and 1900 BCE, across about four thousand seals, tablets and broken pots.",
        "visual_query": "ancient carved stone seal closeup",
        "visual_fallback": "stone seal"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The problem is length. The average inscription runs five signs. Five. There is almost nothing to work with.",
        "visual_query": "terracotta pottery shards archaeology",
        "visual_fallback": "clay shards"
      },
      {
        "narration": "And there is no Rosetta Stone. Not one bilingual text has ever surfaced, and nobody knows what language these cities spoke.",
        "visual_query": "mohenjo daro brick city ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "brick ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "More than a hundred decipherments have been published since the 1920s. The serious contenders split three ways: Dravidian, Indo-Aryan, or, per a 2004 paper, not writing at all. Until a longer text appears, five symbols is all anyone has.",
        "visual_query": "museum artifact display ancient seals",
        "visual_fallback": "museum case"
      }
    ],
